---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { Code } from "astro:components";
title: "Solar Geometry Examples";
description: "Examples demonstrating solar geometry using the Umi‑Time class and public solar helpers.";
---

<Base title="Solar Geometry Examples">
  <div class="umi-region umi-pad">
    <main class="page page-examples-solar">
      <header class="page-header">
        <h1>Solar Geometry Examples</h1>
        <p>
          These examples demonstrate how to compute Layer 3 solar context using
          the <code>UmiTime</code> class and the publicly exported solar-geometry
          helpers. All examples match the current implementation and public API.
        </p>
      </header>

      <!-- UmiTime solar getters -->
      <section class="example">
        <h2>1. Solar Geometry via UmiTime</h2>
        <p>
          The <code>UmiTime</code> class exposes Layer 3 solar-context values as getters.
          These values are derived from solar geometry and require precise latitude
          and longitude to be accurate. Solar context depends on location, but the
          underlying Umi‑Time value does not.
        </p>
        <Code
          code={`import { UmiTime } from "umi-time";

const umi = new UmiTime(
  new Date(),
  { value: 35.14, uncertainty: 0 },     // latitude
  { value: -120.64, uncertainty: 0 }    // longitude
);

console.log(umi.solarDeclination);   // Sun's declination angle
console.log(umi.equationOfTime);     // apparent vs mean solar time
console.log(umi.solarPosition);      // { altitude, azimuth }
console.log(umi.sunrise);            // umi of sunrise
console.log(umi.sunset);             // umi of sunset`}
          lang="js"
          theme="dracula"
        />
      </section>

      <!-- solarAltitude -->
      <section class="example">
        <h2>2. Solar Altitude (Standalone Helper)</h2>
        <p>
          The <code>solarAltitude</code> helper computes the apparent altitude of
          the Sun above the observer's horizon.
        </p>

        <Code
          code={`import { solarAltitude } from "umi-time";

const utc = new Date();
const lat = 35.14;
const lon = -120.64;

const altitude = solarAltitude(utc, lat, lon);
console.log(altitude);  // degrees above horizon`}
          lang="js"
          theme="dracula"
        />
      </section>

      <!-- Hour angle -->
      <section class="example">
        <h2>3. Solar Hour Angle</h2>
        <p>
          The hour angle describes how far the Sun is east or west of physical
          solar noon. It is a fundamental solar-geometry quantity used in the
          computation of sunrise, sunset, twilight, and other solar-context
          values.
        </p>

        <Code
          code={`import { UmiTime, getHourAngle } from "umi-time";

const umi = new UmiTime(
  new Date(),
  { value: 35.14, uncertainty: 0 },
  { value: -120.64, uncertainty: 0 }
);

const eot = umi.equationOfTime;
const utc = umi.utc;
const lon = umi.longitude.value;

const hourAngle = getHourAngle(utc, lon, eot);
console.log(hourAngle);  // degrees`}
          lang="js"
          theme="dracula"
        />
      </section>

      <!-- utUmiFromDate -->
      <section class="example">
        <h2>4. UT Umi From Date</h2>
        <p>
          <code>utUmiFromDate</code> computes the umi position directly from a UTC
          timestamp. This is the Layer 1 Umi‑Time transformation and is the same calculation
          used internally by <code>UmiTime</code>.. Unlike the solar geometry
          helpers, this calculation depends only on UTC and does not require a
          location.
        </p>

        <Code
          code={`import { utUmiFromDate } from "umi-time";

const utc = new Date();
const umiValue = utUmiFromDate(utc);

console.log(umiValue);  // 0–359`}
          lang="js"
          theme="dracula"
        />
      </section>

      <!-- toDeg / toRad -->
      <section class="example">
        <h2>5. Angle Conversion</h2>
        <p>Basic helpers for converting between radians and degrees.</p>

        <Code
          code={`import { toDeg, toRad } from "umi-time";

console.log(toDeg(Math.PI));      // 180
console.log(toRad(180));          // 3.14159...`}
          lang="js"
          theme="dracula"
        />
      </section>

      <!-- Combined example -->
      <section class="example">
        <h2>6. Combined Solar Geometry Example</h2>
        <p>
          A complete example showing how to compute multiple Layer 3
          solar-context values using both <code>UmiTime</code> and standalone solar-geometry
          helpers.
        </p>

        <Code
          code={`import {
  UmiTime,
  solarAltitude,
  getHourAngle,
  toDeg,
  toRad
} from "umi-time";

const umi = new UmiTime(
  new Date(),
  { value: 35.14, uncertainty: 0 },
  { value: -120.64, uncertainty: 0 }
);

// From UmiTime
const decl = umi.solarDeclination;
const eot = umi.equationOfTime;
const pos = umi.solarPosition;

// Standalone helpers
const alt = solarAltitude(umi.utc, umi.latitude.value, umi.longitude.value);
const hourAngle = getHourAngle(umi.utc, umi.longitude.value, eot);

console.log({ decl, eot, pos, alt, hourAngle });`}
          lang="js"
          theme="dracula"
        />
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</Base>
